    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-city capacity rule that rejects owner creation with a **409 Conflict** when the target city already holds 50 or more owners.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`OwnerCityAtCapacityException.java`** (new) \u2014 a dedicated exception carrying the offending city, a client-facing detail message, and the `city` error field. I deliberately kept it separate from the `DuplicateOwnerException` hierarchy since this is a capacity limit, not a uniqueness/duplicate collision, and that base type's contract is explicitly about \"an owner just like this one already exists.\"\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` that maps the new exception to a 409 `ProblemDetail`, mirroring the existing duplicate-owner handler (detail + `errors` array).\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added a `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY = 50` constant and a `rejectCityAtCapacity(...)` guard wired into `addOwner`, placed after the existing duplicate checks. It counts existing owners whose city matches (case-insensitively) and throws when the count is already at or above the cap.\n\nThe V2 controller has no create endpoint (`POST /api/owners` is handled solely by V1), so no change was needed there.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a city to 50, expect the 51st to 409) passes, and the full suite reports `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
